[
    {
        "content": "<p>Greetings! I've come across some slightly strange behavior where an unindented <code>where</code> declaration will cause the next command to not be parsed correctly if it has declaration modifiers above it. A MWE is below:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Problem only if this isn't indented</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Everything works ok without a declModifier (doc comment, attribute, etc.) -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- unexpected token 'def'; expected '(', ':=', '|' or term</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I could not find why this might be happening even after some digging; however, I suspect it may have something to do with how the <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Lean.Parser.Term.whereDecls#doc\">docs#Lean.Parser.Term.whereDecls</a> is implemented, as it has some additional provisions to deal with the optional <code>finally</code> keyword unlike other \"postfix\"-esque syntax (such as <code>deriving</code>, which doesn't suffer from the same issue). </p>\n<p>Lean generally seems pretty permissive of weird indentation practices, but I'm not sure whether it actually intends to support them. As such, I'd be curious to hear y'all's opinion on whether this would be worth opening an issue on Github? (I also do realize that this behavior only crops up in a highly specific situation, so I am unsure whether it is worth maintainers' time). Thank you very much!</p>\n<p>(edits: zulip formatting struggles)</p>",
        "id": 599579669,
        "sender_full_name": "Tate Rowney",
        "timestamp": 1780423229
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>compare with</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- docstring for `fun1.aux2` -/</span>\n<span class=\"n\">aux2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 599589550,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1780425844
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>my guess is that once the parser gets to the <code>def</code> token it has already consumed the docstring as part of another <code>lecRecDecl</code> and won't backtrack when it unexpectedly sees a <code>def</code> token</p>",
        "id": 599590252,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1780426070
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/239415-metaprogramming-.2F-tactics/topic/Strange.20declaration.20parsing.20behavior/near/599589550\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>compare with</p>\n<p><div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- docstring for `fun1.aux2` -/</span>\n<span class=\"n\">aux2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n</code></pre></div><br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Fair enough; but this occurs with quite a few commands besides <code>def</code>. For example, as far as I know something like...</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"n\">aux1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Problem only if this isn't indented</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Everything works ok without a declModifier (doc comment, attribute, etc.) -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myAxiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- unexpected token 'axiom'; expected '(', ':=', '|' or term</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>...couldn't be subsumed into the <code>where</code>.</p>",
        "id": 599590437,
        "sender_full_name": "Tate Rowney",
        "timestamp": 1780426121
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"951211\">Tate Rowney</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/239415-metaprogramming-.2F-tactics/topic/Strange.20declaration.20parsing.20behavior/near/599590437\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Fair enough; but this occurs with quite a few commands besides <code>def</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is a bug in the parser. What Aaron meant to show is a meaningul construct that the parser tries to parse: A second <code>letRecDecl</code> belonging to the <code>where</code>; this time a <code>letRecDecl</code> with a doc comment. But when there is a <code>def</code> or <code>axiom</code>, the second <code>letRecDecl</code> parse fails. As it is defined in <code>Lean.Parser.Term.whereDecls</code>, the parser seems to not backtrack to before the already parsed doc comment consumed by the failed second <code>letRecDecl</code> parse.</p>",
        "id": 599627787,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Miele",
        "timestamp": 1780436679
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you both! I seem to have misinterpreted what Aaron was trying to say. I've opened an <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/issues/13934\">issue</a> on Github as I currently do not have time to work on fixing this myself.</p>",
        "id": 599632880,
        "sender_full_name": "Tate Rowney",
        "timestamp": 1780438558
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think this is the same behavior that makes one-line <code>calc</code> blocks not work very well</p>",
